# Lifecycle Hooks in Unmounting Phase (LWC)

The **Unmounting Phase** is the stage where a Lightning Web Component is being **removed from the DOM**. This phase has exactly one lifecycle hook:

1. `disconnectedCallback()`

---

## disconnectedCallback() Method

> Fires when a component is removed from the DOM.

### Points to Remember
- Fires when a component is removed from the DOM.
- It flows from parent to child.
- This callback method is specific to Lightning Web Components — it isn't from the HTML custom elements specification.

### In Simple Words
Whenever a component is taken off the page — for example, if you conditionally hide it using `if:true` / `if:false`, navigate away from the page, or programmatically remove it — LWC calls `disconnectedCallback()` right before it disappears. This is your chance to **clean up** anything the component was doing, such as:
- Removing event listeners you manually added
- Clearing `setInterval()` / `setTimeout()` timers
- Unsubscribing from a Lightning Message Service channel or pub-sub event
- Releasing any other resources the component was holding onto

### Direction of Flow
Just like `constructor()` and `connectedCallback()`, this hook flows **Parent → Child**. When a parent component (along with its child) is removed from the DOM, the parent's `disconnectedCallback()` fires first, followed by the child's.

---

## 🧩 Complete Working Example — Showing & Hiding a Child Component

This example uses a **toggle button** in the parent to conditionally show/hide the child component using `if:true` / `if:false` directives. Removing the child from the DOM triggers its `disconnectedCallback()`.

### Parent Component (`lifeCycleParent`)

**lifeCycleParent.html**
```html
<template>
    <lightning-card title="Life Cycle Hook Parent Card">
        <div class="slds-m-around_medium">
            <template if:false={isChildVisible}>
                <lightning-button variant="brand" label="Show Child"
                    title="Show Child" onclick={handleclick}
                    class="slds-m-left_x-small"></lightning-button>
            </template>

            <template if:true={isChildVisible}>
                <lightning-button variant="brand" label="Hide Child"
                    title="Hide Child" onclick={handleclick}
                    class="slds-m-left_x-small"></lightning-button>
            </template>
            <template if:true={isChildVisible}>
                <c-life-cycle-child></c-life-cycle-child>
            </template>
        </div>
    </lightning-card>
</template>
```

**lifeCycleParent.js**
```javascript
import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';

export default class LifeCycleParent extends LightningElement {
    isChildVisible = false;
    constructor() {
        super();
        console.log('Parent Constructor Called');
    }
    connectedCallback() {
        console.log('Parent ConnectedCallback Called');
    }
    renderedCallback() {
        console.log('Parent renderedCallback Called');
    }
    handleclick(event) {
        this.isChildVisible = !this.isChildVisible;
    }
}
```

### Child Component (`lifeCycleChild`)

**lifeCycleChild.js**
```javascript
import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';

export default class LifeCycleChild extends LightningElement {
    constructor() {
        super();
        console.log('Child Constructor Called');
    }
    connectedCallback() {
        console.log('Child ConnectedCallback Called');
    }
    renderedCallback() {
        console.log('Child renderedCallback Called');
    }
    disconnectedCallback() {
        alert('Child DisconnectedCallback Called.');
    }
}
```

### 🖥️ How It Behaves

1. **On page load** → `isChildVisible = false`, so the child isn't rendered. Button shows **"Show Child"**.
2. **Click "Show Child"** → `isChildVisible` becomes `true`. The `<c-life-cycle-child>` is inserted into the DOM, so:
   ```
   Child Constructor Called
   Child ConnectedCallback Called
   Child renderedCallback Called
   Parent renderedCallback Called
   ```
3. **Click "Hide Child"** → `isChildVisible` becomes `false`. The `<c-life-cycle-child>` is removed from the DOM, so:
   ```
   Child DisconnectedCallback Called.   (shown as an alert popup)
   Parent renderedCallback Called
   ```

This demonstrates exactly when `disconnectedCallback()` fires — the moment the `if:true` condition turns false and LWC removes the child element from the DOM tree.

